WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.997 --> 00:00:01.824
Hello, everyone!

00:00:01.824 --> 00:00:05.731
It's me again, Artem Vorozhtsov, on the "Mathematical Experiments" channel.

00:00:05.731 --> 00:00:09.288
Today we're going to talk about the Legendre symbol.

00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:11.163
Interestingly, there's no actual symbol.

00:00:11.163 --> 00:00:15.978
Basically, it is denoted as: (m/p)

00:00:15.978 --> 00:00:20.177
It is essentially a function of two variables, m and p, two integers.

00:00:20.177 --> 00:00:27.840
And it is either 1 or -1, and in some generalizations of this symbol, it can also be 0.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.995
What's also interesting is that the generalization is called the Jacobi symbol.

00:00:32.995 --> 00:00:36.954
So, it's like a symbol (supposedly a symbol) that doubts whether it's a symbol.

00:00:36.954 --> 00:00:42.504
Well, rightly so, because this function, which is the Legendre symbol, doesn't have a letter name.

00:00:42.504 --> 00:00:49.609
We have already encountered this symbol (we just didn't call it that) when we drew the multiplication table modulo "p".

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:52.494
Yes, it's about modular arithmetic again.

00:00:52.494 --> 00:00:57.539
You can learn about modular arithmetic in the previous videos.

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:00.511
Here's the multiplication table modulo 17.

00:01:00.511 --> 00:01:07.321
So, the Legendre symbol is equal to one for those numbers m that appear on the diagonal of the multiplication table.

00:01:07.321 --> 00:01:12.735
Well, on the diagonal of the multiplication table, we have squares: 1, 4, 9, 16.

00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:15.704
It should be 25 here, but it's drawn as 8.

00:01:15.704 --> 00:01:22.398
25 modulo 17 is 8. So, the remainder of dividing 25 by 17 is 8.

00:01:22.398 --> 00:01:25.122
These are the squares of numbers modulo "p".

00:01:25.122 --> 00:01:31.789
And for all of them, the Legendre symbol is 1 when "m" is equal to a square,

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:36.315
and for the other numbers, the Legendre symbol is -1.

00:01:36.315 --> 00:01:40.253
We also drew a table of powers.

00:01:40.253 --> 00:01:45.214
Here's a table where the first column consists of solid ones. These are powers of one.

00:01:45.214 --> 00:01:49.404
In the second column, we have powers of two: 2, 4, 8, 16...

00:01:49.404 --> 00:01:52.769
Here, it should be 32, but 32 modulo 17 is 15.

00:01:52.769 --> 00:01:56.110
Here, we have powers of three, four, five, and so on.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:02.510
And we discovered that row number (p-1) in this table consists entirely of ones.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.241
This is Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:02:05.241 --> 00:02:12.431
And row (p-1)/2 consists of numbers 1 and p-1. That was our observation.

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:14.701
So...

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:20.634
Today, I want to tell you a simple thing: that there are three equivalent definitions of the Legendre symbol.

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:28.544
On one hand, the Legendre symbol equals 1 for numbers m that are squares of some number "s."

00:02:28.544 --> 00:02:34.073
On the other hand, it can be defined as this (p-1)/2 row in the table of powers.

00:02:34.073 --> 00:02:39.056
That is, it equals 1 when "m" raised to the power of (p-1)/2 is equal to 1.

00:02:39.056 --> 00:02:41.146
Well, there is a third definition.

00:02:41.146 --> 00:02:48.372
It's the parity of the permutation generated by the row "m" in the multiplication table.

00:02:48.372 --> 00:02:50.537
We'll talk about the parity of permutations.

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:51.654
But let's go in order.

00:02:51.654 --> 00:02:59.779
So, we want to check and, if possible, prove that the answers to these questions are the same.

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:04.754
Either the answer is "yes" for all three questions for number "m", or "no."

00:03:04.754 --> 00:03:06.173
So...

00:03:06.173 --> 00:03:07.612
The diagonal in the multiplication tables.

00:03:07.612 --> 00:03:13.545
Let's draw the multiplication table again and highlight this diagonal.

00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:16.247
Notice that this diagonal is symmetric.

00:03:16.247 --> 00:03:19.562
That is, we have numbers 1, 2, 4, 9, 16...

00:03:19.562 --> 00:03:25.488
And then, the second half of the diagonal repeats this sequence but in a different order.

00:03:25.488 --> 00:03:26.900
Well, no wonder...

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.000
Here we have the squares: 1^2, 2^2, 3^2, 4^2,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.859
And if we go in the other direction, here we have -1^2, -2^2, -3^2, and so on.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:41.108
Let's define this function in the following way.

00:03:41.108 --> 00:03:45.577
The definition may be complex, but the essence of it is as follows:

00:03:45.577 --> 00:03:54.256
For a number "m", there exists a number whose square modulo "p" is precisely "m".

00:03:54.256 --> 00:03:56.793
Let's go step by step.

00:03:56.793 --> 00:04:00.477
This thing here...

00:04:00.477 --> 00:04:08.049
It's simply the squares of all numbers up to (p-1)/2.

00:04:08.049 --> 00:04:14.687
Next, if we take this part of the expression,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:18.587
we obtain squares modulo p.

00:04:18.587 --> 00:04:21.635
If we'll take this expression...

00:04:21.635 --> 00:04:24.628
And write here "equal to m".

00:04:24.628 --> 00:04:27.154
We'll get this peculiar representation.

00:04:27.154 --> 00:04:30.421
And we would like to transform it into another representation.

00:04:30.421 --> 00:04:36.233
Namely: 1 = m or 4 = m, or 9 = m, and so on.

00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:37.730
How can we do that?

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.899
For this, we will perform two operations.

00:04:40.899 --> 00:04:50.000
First, we will simply attach "Thread" to the top.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.906
And the first important event will occur.

00:04:53.906 --> 00:04:57.431
We will, so to speak, embed "m" inside each element.

00:04:57.431 --> 00:05:04.710
"= m" is now included in the list. It's attached to each element of the list.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:09.814
Now we need to replace commas with the conjunction "or".

00:05:09.814 --> 00:05:15.069
This is done as follows: "Or" and "@@"

00:05:15.069 --> 00:05:16.110
Here we go...

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:22.316
Next, we write: if this expression is true, then the answer is 1; otherwise, it's -1.

00:05:22.316 --> 00:05:27.266
That's how we define the Legendre symbol in a concise one-line function.

00:05:27.266 --> 00:05:36.302
Let's draw these Legendre symbols for all numbers from 1 to 16 in the form of a table.

00:05:36.302 --> 00:05:37.852
We've drawn them...

00:05:37.852 --> 00:05:43.901
Now let's look at the row (p - 1)/2 in the table of powers.

00:05:43.901 --> 00:05:46.547
We need to replace 16 with -1, but we'll do more.

00:05:46.547 --> 00:05:51.765
We'll simply replace residues 10, 11, 12, 13, and so on up to 16

00:05:51.765 --> 00:05:57.007
with: 16 becomes -1, 15 becomes -2, 14 becomes -3, and so on.

00:05:57.007 --> 00:06:02.511
Here we have a different definition of the Mod function, which produces these residues.

00:06:02.511 --> 00:06:05.618
Including negative ones.

00:06:05.618 --> 00:06:09.981
Here's the table of powers for this set of residues.

00:06:09.981 --> 00:06:16.866
Let's define the row (p - 1)/2 as the function PowP2.

00:06:16.866 --> 00:06:18.000
Defined.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:26.022
And let's draw these powers as a table.

00:06:26.022 --> 00:06:35.523
Well, this table coincides with the previous one, and that's essentially part of the theorem.

00:06:35.523 --> 00:06:37.939
And next: permutations in the multiplication table.

00:06:37.939 --> 00:06:40.422
Once again, let's draw the multiplication table.

00:06:40.422 --> 00:06:47.394
The multiplication table modulo p is a wonderful thing, where each row and each column contains all numbers from 1 to p-1.

00:06:47.394 --> 00:06:50.571
Therefore, they can be viewed as permutations.

00:06:50.571 --> 00:06:57.832
Here, if we take any row, we can see that the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, ... 16 are sort of shuffled, rearranged.

00:06:57.832 --> 00:07:00.289
A permutation has an important property - parity.

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:02.934
It can be either even or odd.

00:07:02.934 --> 00:07:05.260
How is this property determined?

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.918
It's determined in the following way:

00:07:07.918 --> 00:07:15.004
Any permutation can be represented as a sequence of simple transpositions of two elements.

00:07:15.004 --> 00:07:18.461
Swapping just of two elements is called transposition.

00:07:18.461 --> 00:07:25.213
And any confusion can be created simply by performing permutations of pairs of elements.

00:07:25.213 --> 00:07:30.496
And it's clear that this can be done. Any permutation can be represented as a composition.

00:07:30.496 --> 00:07:37.176
Similarly, it's evident that there are multiple ways to represent a permutation as a composition of transpositions.

00:07:37.176 --> 00:07:43.939
What's not obvious is that the number of transpositions will always remain the same, regardless of how you rearrange them.

00:07:43.939 --> 00:07:50.772
Either it's not the number of transpositions, but the parity of the number of transpositions will always be the same. Either even or odd.

00:07:50.772 --> 00:07:55.290
And the function Signature precisely calculates this parity.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.367
Multiplication by 1 has parity 1,

00:07:57.367 --> 00:07:59.540
multiplication by 2 has parity 1,

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:02.094
multiplication by 3 has parity -1.

00:08:02.094 --> 00:08:07.173
Here, we have computed the parity for all permutations generated by the multiplication table.

00:08:07.173 --> 00:08:10.764
Let's draw it as a table.

00:08:10.764 --> 00:08:15.889
Surprise! It's the same table we've already seen above.

00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:21.857
We discovered this experimentally for the number p = 17.

00:08:21.857 --> 00:08:22.669
Let's check.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:24.947
Signatures are equal to powers.

00:08:24.947 --> 00:08:27.812
Signatures are equal to Legendre symbols.

00:08:27.812 --> 00:08:28.739
True, true.

00:08:28.739 --> 00:08:38.491
The theorem: these three functions coincide for any x from 1 to p-1 for prime numbers p.

00:08:38.491 --> 00:08:45.924
Let's write it as a list of three logical expressions.

00:08:45.924 --> 00:08:49.738
For p = 17, we get True, True, True.

00:08:49.738 --> 00:08:52.092
Let's try 19...

00:08:52.092 --> 00:08:54.668
Let's try 23...

00:08:54.668 --> 00:08:58.283
Well... In general...

00:08:58.283 --> 00:09:04.354
You can try it for various prime numbers and see that it's always true.

00:09:04.354 --> 00:09:07.762
But I don't like to present proofs.

00:09:07.762 --> 00:09:08.688
Try proving it yourself.

00:09:08.688 --> 00:09:17.686
Right now, I want to explain that for non-prime numbers, we get False - Not true.

00:09:17.686 --> 00:09:22.316
And, of course, that's a bit disappointing.

00:09:22.316 --> 00:09:26.900
I would like to generalize the Legendre symbol for non-prime values of p.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.143
Let's try to do that.

00:09:29.143 --> 00:09:35.492
We have already defined the Coprimes function. It is the set of numbers that are coprime with p.

00:09:35.492 --> 00:09:40.429
When p is prime, it is simply all the numbers from 1 to p - 1.

00:09:40.429 --> 00:09:44.851
All the numbers less than p (if p is prime) have no common divisors with it.

00:09:44.851 --> 00:09:45.993
That's clear.

00:09:45.993 --> 00:09:55.002
But if p is non-prime, then only some of the numbers less than p have no common divisors with it.

00:09:55.002 --> 00:10:02.504
Let's redefine our three functions PowP2, SignP and LegendreSymbol

00:10:02.504 --> 00:10:07.619
using the set of coprime numbers, Coprimes.

00:10:07.619 --> 00:10:12.000
Firstly, we will raise to the power not (p - 1)/2,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.821
but to the function (EulerPhi[p])/2.

00:10:14.821 --> 00:10:18.463
The Euler function is the count of numbers coprime with p.

00:10:18.463 --> 00:10:21.909
Simply the size of the Coprimes list.

00:10:21.909 --> 00:10:30.366
We will compute the signature using the multiplication table that only contains numbers coprime with p.

00:10:30.366 --> 00:10:35.515
And we will define the Legendre symbol also using the Coprimes set.

00:10:35.515 --> 00:10:42.000
That is, among the numbers in Coprimes[p], we will look for those whose square equals the number "m".

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.173
We have redefined them.

00:10:44.173 --> 00:10:46.865
And now let's try some challenging cases for p.

00:10:46.865 --> 00:10:49.349
For example, 12.

00:10:49.349 --> 00:10:52.145
Well, we've encountered failure.

00:10:52.145 --> 00:10:57.364
Again, False, False, but True in the middle. And that's interesting.

00:10:57.364 --> 00:10:59.862
Well, how about 129?

00:10:59.862 --> 00:11:01.481
The same result.

00:11:01.481 --> 00:11:03.412
And 9?

00:11:03.412 --> 00:11:04.720
Oops!

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.576
9 is a non-prime number, but we got True three times.

00:11:08.576 --> 00:11:10.149
That's good.

00:11:10.149 --> 00:11:19.705
Let's study the set of numbers for which the Legendre symbol coincides with the PowP2 function.

00:11:19.705 --> 00:11:22.605
Let's run it...

00:11:22.605 --> 00:11:25.159
Computing...

00:11:25.159 --> 00:11:28.200
Here's the result: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7...

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.546
No 8, but 9 is there.

00:11:30.546 --> 00:11:34.530
9 is a non-prime number, 4 is a non-prime number.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.837
Of course, there are prime numbers here.

00:11:37.837 --> 00:11:40.711
But there's something else here, not all numbers though.

00:11:40.711 --> 00:11:43.989
Let's remove all prime numbers.

00:11:43.989 --> 00:11:45.618
Well, the answer is actually written here.

00:11:45.618 --> 00:11:49.648
Let's remove prime numbers and their powers as well.

00:11:49.648 --> 00:11:55.285
After careful observation, we can see that 9 is present...

00:11:55.285 --> 00:11:56.095
No 16 here.

00:11:56.095 --> 00:11:58.683
25...

00:11:58.683 --> 00:12:00.199
27...

00:12:00.199 --> 00:12:03.763
Powers of prime numbers are present here.

00:12:03.763 --> 00:12:08.691
For example, squares of 25 and 49,

00:12:08.691 --> 00:12:15.120
and higher powers. For example, 81 and 125.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:18.920
So, let's say we've uncovered this. Discovered it.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:28.924
And let's see what other numbers there are, among the normal numbers, apart from the powers of prime numbers.

00:12:28.924 --> 00:12:34.579
So, when we consider the complement to the powers of prime numbers,

00:12:34.579 --> 00:12:44.573
we find that there is a set of numbers here, and they are all even, except for one, which doesn't count.

00:12:44.573 --> 00:12:49.179
When we divide these numbers by two,

00:12:49.179 --> 00:12:55.055
we see that after dividing by two, we only have odd numbers.

00:12:55.055 --> 00:12:57.182
We've encountered this before,

00:12:57.182 --> 00:13:02.469
namely when we studied bipedal stick figures in one of the previous videos.

00:13:02.469 --> 00:13:08.440
And these numbers are the numbers for which Gauss Carl Friedrich proved the theorem

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:13.624
that for these numbers, the multiplicative group modulo p is cyclic.

00:13:13.624 --> 00:13:21.035
Well, it seems that we stumbled upon these numbers while studying mere equality.

00:13:21.035 --> 00:13:29.048
The Legendre symbol equals the row of powers with index (EulerPhi[p])/2.

00:13:29.048 --> 00:13:31.429
Great!

00:13:31.429 --> 00:13:34.573
Well, in general, take a look, check it out.

00:13:34.573 --> 00:13:38.405
Here we see either prime numbers or their powers.

00:13:38.405 --> 00:13:44.685
Alright. And here's the second row, which was True in all the cases we tried.

00:13:44.685 --> 00:13:46.708
Let's check it.

00:13:46.708 --> 00:13:51.787
Let's call the numbers for which the second row is true aNumbers.

00:13:51.787 --> 00:13:56.102
So... I wonder, how long will it be calculated.

00:13:56.102 --> 00:13:56.749
Well...

00:13:56.749 --> 00:13:58.625
Here, it seems, all numbers are present.

00:13:58.625 --> 00:14:01.437
Let's check... The length is 400.

00:14:01.437 --> 00:14:09.571
We took 400 numbers. We checked the equality for them, and they all fit.

00:14:09.571 --> 00:14:10.810
Great!

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:14.304
Let's go back to our normal numbers.

00:14:14.304 --> 00:14:17.503
For that, I'd like to see this result explicitly,

00:14:17.503 --> 00:14:24.000
which I mentioned, that normal numbers are numbers that are either equal to a prime number,

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.835
or a power of a prime number, or 2 times a power of a prime number.

00:14:27.835 --> 00:14:30.138
How can we see all of this?

00:14:30.138 --> 00:14:35.835
For that, we will define the function FactorIntegerExpr.

00:14:35.835 --> 00:14:38.700
The FactorInteger function returns a list

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:43.399
that corresponds to pairs like (prime number, power), (prime number, power).

00:14:43.399 --> 00:14:47.648
For example, 15 = 3^1 * 5^1.

00:14:47.648 --> 00:14:50.535
And FactorIntegerExpr does the following:

00:14:50.535 --> 00:14:52.535
Defining...

00:14:54.715 --> 00:14:57.929
This function writes this decomposition in the following form:

00:14:57.929 --> 00:15:04.315
Instead of using powers, it uses subscripts, and instead of multiplication, it represents it as a wedge symbol (Wedge).

00:15:04.315 --> 00:15:11.333
The Wedge function, applied to multiple arguments, becomes an upward-pointing wedge symbol.

00:15:11.333 --> 00:15:13.817
And it is placed between its arguments.

00:15:13.817 --> 00:15:18.227
Let's apply FactorInteger to our normal numbers.

00:15:23.453 --> 00:15:30.674
See, when Wedge is applied to a single object, it is displayed as the word "Wedge",

00:15:30.674 --> 00:15:37.611
because Mathematica can only place the wedge symbol between two expressions.

00:15:37.611 --> 00:15:40.406
Here we see the following:

00:15:40.406 --> 00:15:46.028
Single Wedge symbols are encountered, representing powers of prime numbers.

00:15:46.028 --> 00:15:50.070
Of various powers. There's 3^2, for example.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:53.130
Pairs also encounter.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:58.154
And when it's a pair, the first element in the pair is always 2^1.

00:15:58.154 --> 00:16:02.231
Well, there's also one exception, 2^2.

00:16:02.231 --> 00:16:07.123
Let's sort all these expressions by length.

00:16:07.123 --> 00:16:12.480
First, we have the single Wedge symbols, and then the Wedge symbols for pairs.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.147
We see that there's no threes here,

00:16:14.147 --> 00:16:18.610
because if they were (expressions of length 3), they would be at the end of this list.

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:21.406
Great!

00:16:21.406 --> 00:16:22.610
And...

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:28.935
We also need to check that all expressions of length 2 start with a two, but we'll do that visually.

00:16:28.935 --> 00:16:30.000
Checked.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.134
We can also ask Mathematica to simplify this expression as follows:

00:16:34.134 --> 00:16:38.739
say, Wedge of 2_, y_ (something),

00:16:38.739 --> 00:16:40.636
where the second argument is "something",

00:16:40.636 --> 00:16:44.297
simply becomes Wedge of "something" in any quantity.

00:16:44.297 --> 00:16:48.812
The double underscore "__" indicates a sequence or a list. It doesn't have to be a single element.

00:16:48.812 --> 00:16:55.756
And we'll also eliminate twos raised to the power of one, (2₁), by turning them into ones.

00:16:55.756 --> 00:17:00.725
In fact, although Mathematica is called a functional language,

00:17:00.725 --> 00:17:05.390
in essence, deep down, it's a language of rule-based transformations.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:07.032
And here we have defined two transformation rules

00:17:07.032 --> 00:17:11.524
that will be applied to the expression, simplifying it.

00:17:11.524 --> 00:17:17.077
So, computation in Mathematica is simply the application of rules aimed at simplifying expressions.

00:17:17.077 --> 00:17:23.394
And in the end, sometimes it results in a number, and sometimes it doesn't, and the expression remains complex.

00:17:23.394 --> 00:17:29.169
After this definition, we see exclusively single Wedge symbols.

00:17:29.169 --> 00:17:33.891
We removed 2^1 (2₁), and this is what we ended up with.

00:17:34.791 --> 00:17:36.000
Alright.

00:17:36.519 --> 00:17:40.527
Essentially, we have checked up to n = 400.

00:17:40.527 --> 00:17:45.745
You can check up to a million, and then you'll have to prove it.

00:17:45.745 --> 00:17:48.381
Well... Prove it on your own.

00:17:48.381 --> 00:17:52.457
Here, I took the function from the previous video.

00:17:52.457 --> 00:17:56.395
The function that draws a stick figure for the number p.

00:17:56.395 --> 00:18:01.322
And let's draw stick figures for the first 30 normal numbers.

00:18:01.322 --> 00:18:02.868
And we see that, indeed,

00:18:02.868 --> 00:18:10.578
the number of legs for people representing normal numbers is either 0, 1, or 2.

00:18:10.578 --> 00:18:14.204
There are no four-legged or eight-legged ones.

00:18:14.204 --> 00:18:18.000
There are wings, but exactly two legs.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:22.319
Legs are simply elements that are self-inverse.

00:18:22.319 --> 00:18:30.507
And two pieces mean 1 and -1. Normal numbers have no other elements that are self-inverse.

00:18:30.507 --> 00:18:32.689
Different proofs and ratios.

00:18:32.689 --> 00:18:35.312
I decided to talk about it a little here.

00:18:35.312 --> 00:18:39.273
One of the most important properties of the Legendre symbol is

00:18:39.273 --> 00:18:42.853
that it is a multiplicative function with respect to the first argument.

00:18:42.865 --> 00:18:51.198
If we take "m" as a * b, it is equal to the product of Legendre symbols for a and b.

00:18:51.198 --> 00:19:00.327
And the proof is elementary if we know that the Legendre symbol is equivalent to the power (p-1)/2.

00:19:00.327 --> 00:19:02.085
Alright.

00:19:02.085 --> 00:19:11.006
We will also use the following fact in the proof: a^(p-1) modulo p is equal to one.

00:19:11.006 --> 00:19:17.199
That means the row number (p-1) in the power table is filled with ones.

00:19:17.199 --> 00:19:19.430
How is this proven?

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:21.251
It is proven as follows:

00:19:21.251 --> 00:19:26.677
As we know, multiplying by the number "a" generates a permutation of numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, ..., p-1.

00:19:26.677 --> 00:19:33.280
Let's multiply each of the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, ..., p-1 by "a", and then multiply all these numbers together.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.062
It's clear that the result will be equal to the product of numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, ..., p-1.

00:19:38.062 --> 00:19:41.607
So, we have this identity.

00:19:41.607 --> 00:19:53.043
But when we cancel out 1, 2, 3, 4, and so on, we obtain another identity: 1a * 2a * 3*a ... (p-1)*a = 1 modulo p.

00:19:53.043 --> 00:20:02.152
Thus, we have simply shown that a^(p-1) modulo p is equal to one.

00:20:02.152 --> 00:20:07.289
We used the fact that every element appears in every row of the multiplication table.

00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:10.051
Try proving it yourself.

00:20:10.051 --> 00:20:11.623
Next. The next important step.

00:20:11.623 --> 00:20:18.000
To prove that in the row number (p-1)/2, there can only be 1 or -1.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:22.894
Well, let's denote "x" as m^((p-1)/2).

00:20:22.894 --> 00:20:28.246
Then x^2 = m^(p-1), and we have shown that this is equal to 1.

00:20:28.246 --> 00:20:30.803
So, x^2 is equal to 1.

00:20:30.803 --> 00:20:36.651
From this, it immediately follows that x is either 1 or -1, if we are in a normal field.

00:20:36.651 --> 00:20:43.392
The thing is, (x^2) - 1 can be factored as (x + 1)(x - 1).

00:20:43.392 --> 00:20:51.125
And if we substitute any number other than 1 and -1 into this expression,

00:20:51.125 --> 00:21:00.150
we get a product of numbers that are not zero and not equal to p,

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:05.576
which means their product cannot be zero modulo p.

00:21:05.576 --> 00:21:12.874
So, there are only two possibilities: x = 1 or x = -1.

00:21:12.874 --> 00:21:18.000
Moreover, if we have a number m = a^2 modulo p,

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:23.426
then (x^2) - m, this polynomial, can be factored out into a product of two brackets.

00:21:23.426 --> 00:21:28.009
And if we substitute any number other than "a" and "-a" into these brackets,

00:21:28.009 --> 00:21:29.801
we get a product of two nonzero numbers,

00:21:29.801 --> 00:21:36.473
and their product cannot be zero or divisible by p.

00:21:36.473 --> 00:21:41.445
Here are some important statements written down,

00:21:41.445 --> 00:21:46.525
which I suggest you read and contemplate on.

00:21:46.525 --> 00:21:53.578
And you will come to understand why the three questions posed at the beginning of the video are equivalent to each other.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.000
I also wanted to show you a few numerical experiments with the LegendreSymbol function.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:12.533
I will be using the built-in JacobiSymbol function, which is a generalization of the Legendre symbol.

00:22:12.533 --> 00:22:19.170
You can separately read about what this generalization entails.

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:47.028
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobi_symbol

00:22:47.028 --> 00:22:48.352
Here are the experiments:

00:22:48.352 --> 00:22:54.643
Let's see what the Legendre symbol is for a fixed value of "m", but different prime numbers p.

00:22:54.643 --> 00:22:56.376
For example, let's take 11.

00:22:56.376 --> 00:23:01.075
We see here minus one, ones, and zeros.

00:23:01.075 --> 00:23:03.969
Well, zero happens for the number 11.

00:23:03.969 --> 00:23:08.131
The Jacobi symbol for the case of (p, p) equals 0.

00:23:08.131 --> 00:23:09.822
Well... 11 is too much...

00:23:09.822 --> 00:23:13.207
We need to start with two.

00:23:13.207 --> 00:23:17.371
Then let's introduce three.

00:23:17.371 --> 00:23:18.251
Here we go...

00:23:18.251 --> 00:23:20.251
What do we see here?

00:23:20.251 --> 00:23:23.188
Approximately the same number of ones and minus ones.

00:23:23.188 --> 00:23:26.747
No immediate patterns are visible.

00:23:26.747 --> 00:23:32.023
To discover patterns, let's apply the Accumulate function.

00:23:32.023 --> 00:23:34.369
Specifically, we'll plot the graph not of this function,

00:23:34.369 --> 00:23:39.000
but the graph of the function that represents the sum of the first k elements.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.795
In mathematics, there is a function called Accumulate.

00:23:41.795 --> 00:23:56.253
So, let's plot the Accumulate function of LegendreSymbol for the number m = 11 for the first 500 prime numbers.

00:23:56.253 --> 00:23:57.054
Like this...

00:23:57.054 --> 00:24:01.545
The graph. It can be seen that it goes downwards and reaches the number -20.

00:24:01.545 --> 00:24:11.401
So, -20 means that in the first 500 prime numbers, there were 20 more minus ones than ones.

00:24:11.401 --> 00:24:13.836
Okay. And for two?

00:24:13.836 --> 00:24:15.998
The graph also goes downwards.

00:24:15.998 --> 00:24:18.656
Here it reaches -16.

00:24:18.656 --> 00:24:21.474
For three...

00:24:21.474 --> 00:24:22.021
Here.

00:24:22.021 --> 00:24:27.539
For three, around the 500 mark, the graph returns to 0.

00:24:27.539 --> 00:24:30.203
Accumulate allows us to check the balance between ones and minus ones.

00:24:30.203 --> 00:24:31.546
It's a simple idea, really.

00:24:31.546 --> 00:24:32.715
Four.

00:24:32.715 --> 00:24:33.979
Ah! Well, with four, it's all clear.

00:24:33.979 --> 00:24:36.000
Four is a square.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:40.341
And therefore, for large prime numbers, the answer is clearly 1.

00:24:40.341 --> 00:24:43.742
And for 500, we get 500.

00:24:43.742 --> 00:24:45.021
Alright.

00:24:45.021 --> 00:24:46.190
Moving on.

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:48.000
Number 5.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:49.470
Downwards.

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:52.232
Number 6.

00:24:52.232 --> 00:24:54.543
Number 7.

00:24:54.543 --> 00:24:56.543
Alright...

00:24:56.543 --> 00:25:00.566
Quite different graphs. No apparent patterns.

00:25:00.566 --> 00:25:05.323
Let's see what the Jacobi symbol equals in the case

00:25:05.323 --> 00:25:10.610
when we consider not only prime numbers, but also composite ones. (It makes sense to consider only odd numbers p)

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:15.966
Interesting. For 11, we get fixed values of -4 and 2.

00:25:15.966 --> 00:25:19.754
So Accumulate stays within the vicinity of zero.

00:25:19.754 --> 00:25:23.576
It provides answers within a small range.

00:25:23.576 --> 00:25:25.034
Let me try 5...

00:25:25.034 --> 00:25:28.003
There are three possible values for the Accumulate function.

00:25:28.003 --> 00:25:29.827
Let me try 2...

00:25:29.827 --> 00:25:33.961
Let me try 127...

00:25:33.961 --> 00:25:37.984
Well, this one's a bit more complicated. -10, 10.

00:25:37.984 --> 00:25:47.044
But it's evident that the accumulation of the Jacobi symbol is a more regular thing

00:25:47.044 --> 00:25:51.681
than the accumulation of the Legendre symbol using only prime numbers.

00:25:51.681 --> 00:25:53.689
Alright...

00:25:53.689 --> 00:25:56.588
Let's go back to prime numbers,

00:25:56.588 --> 00:26:03.364
but now let's consider the Jacobi symbol for (p-1)/2.

00:26:06.825 --> 00:26:08.935
Here's what we get:

00:26:08.935 --> 00:26:16.579
Around ten thousand, we return to zero, but what happens beyond that is unclear.

00:26:16.579 --> 00:26:23.654
Let's take a look at Accumulate for the case when we take the Euler function as "m".

00:26:23.654 --> 00:26:25.775
It should actually...

00:26:25.775 --> 00:26:26.586
But no...

00:26:26.586 --> 00:26:30.206
It shouldn't match. If we divide by 2, then it will match.

00:26:30.206 --> 00:26:33.416
Yes, it matches in this variant.

00:26:33.416 --> 00:26:40.020
And without dividing by 2, it's simply -1 for prime numbers.

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:46.199
So, the accumulation of the Legendre symbol for the number m = -1.

00:26:46.199 --> 00:26:50.199
Let's continue experimenting...

00:26:50.199 --> 00:26:52.568
These are only odd numbers that I took.

00:26:52.568 --> 00:26:56.575
I looked at the variant (p-1)/2.

00:26:56.575 --> 00:26:58.494
And further...

00:26:58.494 --> 00:26:59.029
Like this...

00:26:59.029 --> 00:27:00.925
This is already an interesting answer...

00:27:00.925 --> 00:27:02.960
I'll hide this for now.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:09.787
Let's calculate the Jacobi symbol for the number -1, for a large number 150,000.

00:27:09.787 --> 00:27:15.074
Here I accidentally took all numbers, not just the odd ones. If we take only the even numbers, the accumulation result is either 0 or 1.

00:27:15.074 --> 00:27:18.208
JacobySymbol[m,p] function, simply discards the powers of two in the number p.

00:27:19.718 --> 00:27:24.810
It's evident that it grows linearly, but not with a coefficient of 1.

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:28.634
Here, I tried fitting and it seemed like the coefficient is 1/4.

00:27:28.634 --> 00:27:31.567
Let's check...

00:27:31.567 --> 00:27:33.043
Yes! Indeed.

00:27:33.043 --> 00:27:36.428
When we take the Jacobi symbol,

00:27:36.428 --> 00:27:41.300
accumulate it, and subtract n/4,

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:45.469
we obtain a function that grows very slowly, or might not grow at all.

00:27:45.469 --> 00:27:49.637
And sometimes it jumps to the value 15.

00:27:49.637 --> 00:27:50.572
So...

00:27:50.572 --> 00:27:57.590
Now let's generate a set of numbers that are powers of prime numbers,

00:27:57.590 --> 00:28:00.720
as well as a set of prime numbers.

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:09.339
And let's plot this plot again, limiting it to the set of powers of prime numbers.

00:28:09.339 --> 00:28:11.414
Plotting...

00:28:11.414 --> 00:28:12.341
So...

00:28:12.341 --> 00:28:15.506
Here, I also drew some kind of answer...

00:28:15.506 --> 00:28:19.825
Initially, the graph looked like this,

00:28:19.825 --> 00:28:22.240
which jumped over 150.

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:30.849
But then I decided to subtract the square root of "n" divided by Pi^2.

00:28:30.849 --> 00:28:35.536
And I got a plot that jumps around zero.

00:28:35.536 --> 00:28:45.005
But if we only consider the powers of odd prime numbers,

00:28:45.005 --> 00:28:50.708
the situation will look better.

00:28:50.708 --> 00:28:51.623
Here...

00:28:51.623 --> 00:28:56.270
At the beginning, the plot hovers around zero,

00:28:56.270 --> 00:29:02.700
while here, it starts going upward and doesn't hover around zero.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:07.498
So, excluding the powers of two from the powers set

00:29:07.498 --> 00:29:13.201
gives a more regular, normal picture for the plot.

00:29:13.201 --> 00:29:20.219
Of course, it's chaotic, but you can see that the graph hovers around zero at the beginning and later on.

00:29:20.219 --> 00:29:25.805
Here, there is some normalization on the X-axis, taking the logarithm,

00:29:25.805 --> 00:29:31.081
and on the Y-axis, dividing by the logarithm of the number.

00:29:31.081 --> 00:29:32.735
This is the resulting picture.

00:29:32.735 --> 00:29:37.019
Let's zoom more...

00:29:37.019 --> 00:29:39.815
Here we go...

00:29:39.815 --> 00:29:41.827
Why am I showing all this?

00:29:41.827 --> 00:29:48.418
Well, try to prove that this function

00:29:48.418 --> 00:29:57.478
(the Jacobian symbol applied to the powers of prime numbers without powers of two) minus ((the square root of n) divided by Pi^2)

00:29:57.478 --> 00:30:02.073
crosses the X-axis an infinite number of times

00:30:02.073 --> 00:30:06.000
and moves away from the X-axis no more than...

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:10.180
Well, here you have to come up with how much yourself.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:11.862
Moving on.

00:30:11.862 --> 00:30:16.653
Earlier, we studied m = -1.

00:30:16.653 --> 00:30:19.034
Well, not really studied, just drew one plot.

00:30:19.034 --> 00:30:23.387
And here, instead of -1, I plotted the EulerPhi function,

00:30:23.387 --> 00:30:24.579
and voila!

00:30:24.579 --> 00:30:31.285
Instead of subtracting, now we need to add the square root of the number divided by Pi^2.

00:30:31.285 --> 00:30:38.257
And in this case, we get a regular pattern that hovers around zero.

00:30:38.257 --> 00:30:39.933
Next.

00:30:39.933 --> 00:30:42.660
What happens if we apply the Euler function twice?

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:45.871
Well, it's just some kind of alchemy.

00:30:45.871 --> 00:30:49.462
We get this growing function.

00:30:49.462 --> 00:30:54.000
Resembling either a square root or something else.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.741
I suggest you explore it.

00:30:55.741 --> 00:31:08.249
In other words, EulerPhi of EulerPhi gives a value of m where ones strongly dominate over minus ones.

00:31:08.249 --> 00:31:09.591
Why is that?

00:31:09.591 --> 00:31:11.280
We need to figure it out...

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:13.406
Let's apply the same trick here.

00:31:13.406 --> 00:31:17.827
Let's only consider odd powers.

00:31:17.827 --> 00:31:24.465
And let's try to fit a function consisting of ones, x, and square root of x with some coefficients.

00:31:24.465 --> 00:31:28.875
Here, it's fitted.

00:31:28.875 --> 00:31:34.754
If we fit the version without excluding the powers of two, we get a slightly different result,

00:31:34.754 --> 00:31:39.626
but what appears next to the square root of X is approximately the same...

00:31:39.626 --> 00:31:43.310
2.101... What is this constant? That's the question.

00:31:43.310 --> 00:31:46.251
Well, let's draw...

00:31:46.251 --> 00:31:47.651
This is what we get...

00:31:47.651 --> 00:31:56.183
So, subtracting this function from the accumulation of the Jacobian symbol for powers of prime numbers

00:31:56.183 --> 00:32:03.305
gives a regular pattern that oscillates around zero.

00:32:03.305 --> 00:32:09.604
Alright... now I need larger steps of prime numbers.

00:32:09.604 --> 00:32:17.314
You can try this yourself in your own program.

00:32:17.314 --> 00:32:26.580
And there's also the Möbius function, which is interesting to investigate in the same way.

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:30.587
We'll delve into that in the near future.

00:32:30.587 --> 00:32:32.242
That's all for today!

00:32:32.242 --> 00:32:35.314
